<!--
Conventions:
- BORN TIERED. The ledger has a HOT `## Frontier` (open units + the recent-
  closed window + any closed unit a still-open unit still cites as an input) and
  a COLD `## Archive` (everything older, one line each). The driver re-reads the
  frontier every turn and the archive never — that is what keeps per-turn read
  cost flat as the project scales. Read the AUDIT FILE, not a fat ledger cell.
- SINGLE WRITER. Only the driver writes this file. Workers execute one unit and
  return a terse structured result (status + output pointer + index facts); the
  driver — the single writer — records it. This kills the v2 dup-row race by
  construction (no second context ever writes back). See docs/03-work-as-a-dag.md.
- IDs are flat (U1, U2, U3...). No prefixes for hierarchy. The DAG's structure
  lives in the `Inputs` edges, not in the IDs.
- Each row produces ONE deliverable (atomicity rule). A module + its test file
  count as ONE unit (the natural grain) — atomicity is one deliverable, not
  literally one file.
- OUTPUT CELL = the machine's OWNERSHIP KEY (parsed by the immutability hook), so
  it is a validated micro-schema, not free prose:
    · Concrete backticked path(s) ONLY — `src/thing.ts`. NO globs, NO directories
      (`pipelines/`, `{server,client}/src`, `foo/*.ts`): a directory Output would
      over-freeze every file under it, incl. new/untracked ones, and a glob
      realpaths to a literal that matches no concrete file, so it never enters the
      ownership set. List the actual files.
    · Multi-file deliverable → COMMA-SEPARATED backticks: `a.ts`, `b.ts`. The hook
      parses EVERY backticked path, so the unit owns (freezes when closed / keeps
      editable when an active follow-on) ALL of them, not just the first.
    · NO raw `|` in ANY cell — a literal pipe shifts the columns and breaks the
      positional parse. Use `/` or escape it. (The hook fails safe on a mis-split
      row: it freezes nothing for that row rather than trust a shifted index.)
- `Kind` types the unit so the audit picks the right verified-done dimensions.
  Values (default `authoring`):
    authoring     — code/docs; the standard 7-point audit.
    ui            — a rendered/visual artifact; audit rebuilds from CURRENT source
                    + objective machine checks (DOM/bbox/counts), no reliance on
                    worker prose about visuals.
    eval          — a measurement/judge/comparison; audit checks HARNESS FAIRNESS
                    (equal-fidelity inputs, symmetric yardstick, A/B-swap for
                    position bias, confounds controlled/labelled).
    deploy-infra  — a deployed/stateful resource; audit exercises it end-to-end AS
                    the deployed identity (a hardcoded identity/endpoint is a finding).
    data          — a produced dataset/table; audit re-queries it as the downstream
                    consumer.
    diagnosis     — a "why is X broken" unit; audit requires a CONTROLLED
                    reproduction (strip model/harness/config confounds) before any
                    root-cause is accepted.
- `Tier` is a STATIC PER-UNIT LABEL — the worker's model tier — the driver sets
  by the rubric at plan time, like `Kind`. Values (default `standard`):
    economy   — small/fast model, low effort — mechanical extraction/reformat.
    standard  — mid model, medium effort — routine authoring/data. (default)
    frontier  — strong model, high effort — diagnosis/design/keystone/irreversible.
  `Kind` largely PREDICTS Tier (eval/deploy-infra/diagnosis → frontier;
  ui/mechanical → economy); the exception is `authoring`, which splits on
  keystone-ness — so `Tier` stays its OWN column, not a value derived from `Kind`.
  The runtime binds `tier → (model, effort)` and sets it at spawn (the worker
  never reads its own tier). It is NOT a cost governor / budget scheduler — just a
  static difficulty label; escalation (kill-and-respawn one tier up on audit-FAIL)
  handles a mis-tiered unit at run time. See docs/05 model tiering.
- `Inputs` lists prior units whose outputs this one consumes — the dependency
  edges of the DAG. A unit is dispatchable only once every input is closed and
  past its audit gate (foundation = upstream topology, not a reminder).
- Status — the lifecycle, FIVE states:
    pending        — born, not yet dispatched (a leaf of the ready-set once
                     its inputs clear).
    in_progress    — a worker is executing it (or it is mid-recovery).
    authored-done  — a worker produced the output; the artifact EXISTS but has
                     NOT been independently checked. NOT consumable downstream
                     on its own — the producing worker never self-certifies.
    verified-done  — an INDEPENDENT audit/verification worker (auditor ≠ author)
                     checked the output and it passed. Only a separate, driver-
                     launched worker confers this; for deploy/infra/data units
                     the worker also exercises the live artifact as its real
                     consumer. This is the consumable terminal state.
    superseded     — scope changed; the unit was replaced by a follow-on.
- `Audit` holds the verdict + a pointer: `—` (n/a) | `pending` (authored-done,
  awaiting an auditor) | `PASS→audits/U<id>.md` | `PASS-FINDINGS→audits/U<id>.md`
  | `FAIL→audits/U<id>.md` | `superseded by U<yy>`. A unit reaches
  `verified-done` only on `PASS` / `PASS-FINDINGS`; `FAIL` keeps it blocking.
  A downstream unit may CONSUME an input only when that input is `verified-done`
  (Audit `PASS` / `PASS-FINDINGS`). One audit + one report PER UNIT, from its
  OWN report — by a DIFFERENT worker than the author. See PROTOCOL.md -> Audit
  gates.
- BORN pending/in_progress, NEVER done. A new unit (incl. a fix or follow-on)
  is registered `pending`/`in_progress`; flip to `authored-done` only after its
  output exists, and to `verified-done` only after an independent worker passes
  it (crash-safe ordering; avoids the immutability hook locking you out of the
  file you are producing). See PROTOCOL.md -> Resilience.
- Closed units are never edited. To revise, append a follow-on unit.
- COMPACTION is an index op, not a freeze violation. At session-start reconcile,
  when the ledger crosses a size/count threshold, move aged-out closed+verified
  units from `## Frontier` to a one-line `## Archive` entry, and roll `Recent
  updates` past its window into the archive too. This is LOSSLESS — the audit
  pointer is kept, the detail already lives in the output/`audits/`/`DECISIONS.md`
  — so it does not touch any frozen output and is NOT an immutability violation.
- Stamp `last_updated` (front matter) on every write-back, so the ledger's age
  is honest.
- ONE recovery log, ONE ordering: `## Recent updates` is the single recovery log
  and is newest-first; `## Active unit` is a one-line pointer, never a dated
  banner stack. A second de-facto log forming is a reconciliation smell.
  See PROTOCOL.md -> Resilience.
- A frontier row is also machine-parsed (the immutability hook reads it by
  COLUMN NAME to decide what to freeze). Keep every `| U<n> |` row well-formed —
  correct column count (matching the header), a valid status, a parseable Output
  cell of concrete backticked path(s) — so a botched edit can't silently change
  what the hook freezes. The hook parses ALL backticked Output paths (multi-file
  ownership) and FAILS SAFE on a mis-split row (freezes nothing for it rather than
  trust a shifted index). See PROTOCOL.md -> Anti-drift & the freeze / Resilience.
- LOCKSTEP CAVEAT — a column change shifts positional indices. Any runtime parser
  that reads ledger columns BY POSITION (the immutability hook, the compaction
  script) MUST be updated in the SAME change that lands this `Tier` column, and
  RUN against a real ledger — inserting a column silently re-numbers every column
  after it, so a stale positional parser reads the wrong cells (freezes the wrong
  path, mis-reads Status). This is the v3.35 ledger-shape lesson: never ship a
  column move without its parser update + a real-ledger parse test.
- ARCHIVE ENTRIES ARE ALSO MACHINE-PARSED, AND IN ONE CANONICAL SHAPE. The
  `## Archive` cold tier is a one-line BULLET per unit — never a table — in this
  exact form (fields separated by " · "; an adopter's immutability hook,
  compaction script, and v2->v3 converter all read it):
      - U<n> — <title> · `<output/path>` · <status> · <verdict>→audits/U<n>.md
  Every archive bullet MUST carry a parseable Status (the 3rd " · " field) and a
  backticked Output (the path) so the hook can still freeze an aged-out closed
  unit's output. A `verified-done` / `authored-done` / legacy `done` archive
  bullet IS frozen exactly like a frontier row; a `superseded` one is not. Keep
  the status token verbatim — don't downgrade `verified-done` to `done`.
- Off-mission artifacts (work that does not serve the mission) do NOT go in the
  Frontier table. Produce the file, then add a "Recent updates" line marked
  `(not a mission unit)`. See PROTOCOL.md -> Off-mission artifacts.
- PLATFORM BINDING: this template uses generic paths ("the store", `audits/`,
  `LEDGER-ARCHIVE`). The concrete layout — e.g. a `.gotm/` subfolder, the split-
  file convention, a compaction script — is an adopter/platform binding, not part
  of the framework. Adjust the relative paths to your layout.
